1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,430
Good morning, everyone.

2
00:00:01,430 --> 00:00:07,050
I've been invited to talk about my research project into Australian Aboriginal rock paintings.

3
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:12,551
The Australian Aborigines have recorded both real and symbolic images of their time on

4
00:00:12,551 --> 00:00:14,820
rock walls for many thousands of years.

5
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:19,721
Throughout the long history of this tradition, new images have appeared

6
00:00:19,721 --> 00:00:21,770
and new painting styles have developed.

7
00:00:21,770 --> 00:00:27,060
And these characteristics can be used to categorise the different artistic styles.

8
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:32,950
Among these are what we call the Dynamic, Yam and Modern styles of painting.

9
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:38,620
One of the most significant characteristics of the different styles is the way that

10
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:40,550
humans are depicted in the paintings.

11
00:00:40,550 --> 00:00:44,770
The more recent paintings show people in static poses.

12
00:00:44,770 --> 00:00:51,165
But the first human images to dominate rock art paintings, over 8,000 years ago,

13
00:00:51,165 --> 00:00:52,170
were full of movement.

14
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:57,672
These paintings showed people hunting and cooking food and so they were given the

15
00:00:57,672 --> 00:00:59,800
name 'Dynamic' to reflect this energy.

16
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:04,800
It's quite amazing considering they were painted in such a simple stick-like form.

17
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:09,904
In the Yam period, there was a movement away from stick figures to a

18
00:01:09,904 --> 00:01:11,110
more naturalistic shape.

19
00:01:11,110 --> 00:01:14,599
However, they didn't go as far as the Modern style,

20
00:01:14,599 --> 00:01:18,871
which is known as 'x-ray' because it actually makes a feature of the internal

21
00:01:18,871 --> 00:01:22,140
skeleton as well as the organs of animals and humans.

22
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:26,652
The Yam style of painting got its name from the fact that it featured

23
00:01:26,652 --> 00:01:30,884
much curvier figures that actually resemble the vegetable called a yam,

24
00:01:30,884 --> 00:01:33,050
which is similar to a sweet potato.

25
00:01:33,050 --> 00:01:38,231
The Modern paintings are interesting because they include paintings at the time of the

26
00:01:38,231 --> 00:01:40,440
first contact with European settlers.

27
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:46,176
Aborigines managed to convey the idea of the settlers' clothing by simply painting the

28
00:01:46,176 --> 00:01:51,029
Europeans without any hands, indicating the habit of standing with their hands in their

29
00:01:51,029 --> 00:01:51,490
pockets!

30
00:01:51,490 --> 00:01:54,138
Size is another characteristic.

31
00:01:54,138 --> 00:01:58,097
The more recent images tend to be life size or even larger,

32
00:01:58,097 --> 00:02:01,400
but the Dynamic figures are painted in miniature.

33
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:08,600
Aboriginal rock art also records the environmental changes that occurred over thousands of years.

34
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:15,959
For example, we know from the Dynamic paintings that over 8,000 years ago, Aborigines would have rarely eaten fish

35
00:02:15,959 --> 00:02:19,270
and sea levels were much lower at this time.

36
00:02:19,270 --> 00:02:25,694
In fact, fish didn't start to appear in paintings until the Yam period along with shells

37
00:02:25,694 --> 00:02:27,300
and other marine images.

38
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:33,024
The paintings of the Yam tradition also suggest that, during this time, the Aborigines

39
00:02:33,024 --> 00:02:38,747
moved away from animals as their main food source and began including vegetables in

40
00:02:38,747 --> 00:02:41,200
their diet, as these feature prominently.

41
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:49,319
Freshwater creatures didn't appear in the paintings until the Modern period from 4,000 years

42
00:02:49,319 --> 00:02:49,860
ago.

43
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:54,313
So, these paintings have already taught us a lot.

44
00:02:54,313 --> 00:03:01,240
But one image that has always intrigued us is known as the 'Rainbow Serpent'.

45
00:03:01,240 --> 00:03:02,305
The Rainbow Serpent,

46
00:03:02,305 --> 00:03:07,633
which is the focus of my most recent project, gets its name from its snake

47
00:03:07,633 --> 00:03:12,960
or serpent-like body and it first appeared in the Yam period 4 to 6,000

48
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:13,670
years ago.

49
00:03:13,670 --> 00:03:18,830
Many believe it is a curious mixture of kangaroo, snake and crocodile.

50
00:03:18,830 --> 00:03:23,294
But we decided to study the Rainbow Serpent paintings to see if we could

51
00:03:23,294 --> 00:03:27,120
locate the animal that the very first painters based their image on.

52
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:32,600
The Yam period coincided with the end of the last ice age.

53
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:37,800
This brought about tremendous change in the environment, with the sea levels rising

54
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:39,400
and creeping steadily inland.

55
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:44,835
This flooded many familiar land features and also caused a great deal of disruption

56
00:03:44,835 --> 00:03:47,940
to traditional patterns of life, hunting in particular.

57
00:03:47,940 --> 00:03:53,624
New shores were formed and totally different creatures would have washed up onto the

58
00:03:53,624 --> 00:03:54,030
shores.

59
00:03:54,030 --> 00:03:59,448
We studied 107 paintings of the Rainbow Serpent and found that the one creature

60
00:03:59,448 --> 00:04:02,931
that matches it most closely was the Ribboned Pipefish,

61
00:04:02,931 --> 00:04:05,640
which is a type of sea horse.

62
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:12,781
This sea creature would have been a totally unfamiliar sight in the inland regions where the image is found

63
00:04:12,781 --> 00:04:16,540
and may have been the inspiration behind the early paintings.

64
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:21,643
So, at the end of the ice age there would have been enormous changes in animal

65
00:04:21,643 --> 00:04:22,600
and plant life.

66
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:24,288
It's not surprising then,

67
00:04:24,288 --> 00:04:29,350
that the Aborigines linked this abundance to the new creatures they witnessed.

68
00:04:29,350 --> 00:04:34,120
Even today, Aborigines see the Rainbow Serpent as a symbol of creation,

69
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:39,685
which is understandable given the increase in vegetation and the new life forms that

70
00:04:39,685 --> 00:04:42,070
featured when the image first appeared.
